#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Mewo Session for Linux: the sessions of "Build with me", where you watch
them.

The chat (v3.3): one window for every session, a tab each, as the Claude
Code extension shows its conversations. A session is not the window's: its
host (mewo-session-host, beside this file) runs Claude Code and keeps the
conversation, and the window connects to it (`mewo-session --attach
<host socket>`). The first host to start opens the window; the next ones
find it on its socket and become tabs. A window that freezes, is force quit
or is closed with "Keep them running" leaves the sessions working; the next
window, which opens with any new session or the console's "Show the
session", shows each live one again, its whole conversation included.
Closing a tab, or the window with "Stop them", ends the session. The page
beside this file, mewo-session-chat.html, draws each conversation the way
the extension does: messages, tool calls with input and output, diffs,
questions and permission prompts as buttons, a composer with the model and
the mode. Every tab's page runs in one WebKit, which draws without the GPU:
WebKitGTK drawing through it hangs on NVIDIA under Wayland once a few
sessions are open.

The terminal (GTK + VTE, `mewo-session <info.json>`): Claude Code as it is
in a terminal, in the console's colours, a window per session; it is what
opens without WebKitGTK or with `session_terminal`. The header follows the
session in both: its state (working, needs you, finished), its plan status
and its pull request.

In the terminal, Ctrl+C copies the selection (with nothing selected it reaches Claude as
usual), Ctrl+V pastes text (an image on the clipboard goes to Claude, which
pastes it itself), Ctrl+Shift+C / Ctrl+Shift+V always copy and paste, a
right click offers Copy, Paste and Select all; Ctrl+= / Ctrl+- / Ctrl+0 zoom.
In the chat, Ctrl+W closes the tab and Ctrl+Page Up / Page Down move between tabs.
"""

